/-
# The umbral language, and Rota's substitution principle as a free theorem

The coefficient ring, the umbrae and the umbral algebra are type variables of
System F, and their structure is supplied as a **class constraint**:

    ∀K U A.  Umbral K U A  ⇒  U → K

A class constraint in System F is dictionary passing, and the free theorem of a
constrained type is the free theorem of the dictionary-passing type with the
hypothesis that the two dictionaries are *related*. That hypothesis is `Respects`
below, and it is the whole mechanism: each clause of `Respects` is one class
member, and the clause for `evalA` is where the mathematics happens.

## What comes out

* `similarity` — **Rota's substitution principle.** Similar umbrae are
  interchangeable in every definable scalar-valued umbral expression. The
  relation at the umbra variable is arbitrary; the only thing constraining it is
  that the dictionaries be related.

* `substitution` — the same principle for an expression in any number of
  umbrae, at `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → ⋯ → U → K`: a similar umbra may be
  substituted provided it does not already occur.

* `moments_of_common_target` and `no_common_target` — **not** Rota's uncorrelation axiom, but
  what that axiom costs. Uncorrelation is *definitional* in the moment model:
  `eval` is defined as a sum over monomials of a product over distinct
  variables, so there is nothing to derive and no free theorem to derive it
  from. What *is* derived is the restriction uncorrelation imposes: a relation
  respecting the dictionary that relates two distinct umbrae to one forces
  `E[aⁱ] E[cʲ] = E[b^{i+j}]`, so it cannot do so for umbrae of nonzero variance.
  The `evalA` clause of `Respects` refuses it, because `eval` is multiplicative
  across distinct umbrae and not across a repeated one.

* `exchangeable` — at `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → U → K`, an expression in two
  similar umbrae is symmetric in them.

* `integral` — at a relation that is not a graph (the diagonal of the subring
  generated by the moments), every expression is an integer polynomial in the
  moments.

## What "theorem" means here, exactly

In each case the universal quantifier over expressions is what parametricity
supplies, and the content about `eval` is supplied by the moment model. For
`similarity` the content is `eval_rename`, an ordinary induction in
`Umbral/Moments.lean`. It is not a free theorem, and it need not be: what the substitution principle asserts is a
statement about *every* umbral expression, and that quantifier is the part
obtained for nothing. `Umbral/Naturality.lean` explains why, at this type, the
free theorem amounts to naturality along homomorphisms of dictionaries.
-/

import FreeThms.Core.SystemF
import FreeThms.Umbral.Moments
import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing

namespace FreeThms.Umbral.Restricted

open FreeThms

/-! ## The language

The types of the language mention no base sorts. Three base sorts occur in the
semantics only, as the sets at which the three type variables are instantiated:
the coefficient ring, the umbrae, and the umbral algebra. -/

/-- The three slots a client instantiates. -/
inductive Slot
  | K
  | U
  | A
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

/-- The base denotation, one type per slot. -/
abbrev baseD (K U A : Type) : Slot → Type
  | .K => K
  | .U => U
  | .A => A

open PTy in
/--
The class constraint `Umbral K U A`, as a curried dictionary.

Under the three quantifiers of `oneUmbraTy` the de Bruijn indices are `K = 2`,
`U = 1`, `A = 0`, the outermost binder having the largest index.

The members are the ring operations of the coefficients, the ring operations of
the umbral algebra, and the three structure maps: the inclusion of coefficients,
the inclusion of umbrae, and the evaluation.
-/
def dictTy (body : PTy 3) : PTy 3 :=
  tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2 ⇛ (tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2) ⇛ (tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2) ⇛
    (tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 2) ⇛
  tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0 ⇛ (tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0) ⇛ (tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0) ⇛
    (tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 0) ⇛
  (tvar 2 ⇛ tvar 0) ⇛ (tvar 1 ⇛ tvar 0) ⇛ (tvar 0 ⇛ tvar 2) ⇛ body

open PTy in
/-- `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ body`. The body is a parameter, so one quantifier
prefix and one written-out free theorem serve every body. -/
abbrev quantTy (body : PTy 3) : PTy 0 :=
  .all (.all (.all (dictTy body)))

open PTy in
/-- `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → K`: a scalar-valued umbral expression in one
umbra, over an arbitrary coefficient ring and an arbitrary umbral algebra. -/
abbrev oneUmbraTy : PTy 0 := quantTy (tvar 1 ⇛ tvar 2)

/-! ## The dictionary, semantically -/

/-- What the class `Umbral K U A` provides. -/
structure Dict (K U A : Type) where
  /-- Zero of the coefficients. -/ zeroK : K
  /-- One of the coefficients. -/ oneK : K
  /-- Addition of coefficients. -/ addK : K → K → K
  /-- Multiplication of coefficients. -/ mulK : K → K → K
  /-- Negation of coefficients. -/ negK : K → K
  /-- Zero of the umbral algebra. -/ zeroA : A
  /-- One of the umbral algebra. -/ oneA : A
  /-- Addition in the umbral algebra. -/ addA : A → A → A
  /-- Multiplication in the umbral algebra. -/ mulA : A → A → A
  /-- Negation in the umbral algebra. -/ negA : A → A
  /-- A coefficient is a constant of the algebra. -/ constA : K → A
  /-- An umbra is an element of the algebra. -/ injA : U → A
  /-- The evaluation functional. -/ evalA : A → K

/-- Read a closed term at a coefficient ring, a set of umbrae, an umbral algebra
and a dictionary. -/
def den (K U A : Type) (d : Dict K U A) (t : PClosedTm oneUmbraTy) :
    U → K :=
  pevalC (baseD K U A) t (.base .K) (.base .U) (.base .A)
    d.zeroK d.oneK d.addK d.mulK d.negK
    d.zeroA d.oneA d.addA d.mulA d.negA
    d.constA d.injA d.evalA

/-! ## When two dictionaries are related

This is the class side-condition of the free theorem at a constrained type. One
clause per class member. -/

/--
**A triple of relations respects the umbral structure.**

The `evalA` clause is the load-bearing one: it says the evaluation cannot tell
related elements of the algebra apart. Everything limitative in this file comes
from trying and failing to satisfy it.
-/
structure Respects {K₁ U₁ A₁ K₂ U₂ A₂ : Type}
    (d₁ : Dict K₁ U₁ A₁) (d₂ : Dict K₂ U₂ A₂)
    (R : K₁ → K₂ → Prop) (S : U₁ → U₂ → Prop) (T : A₁ → A₂ → Prop) : Prop where
  /-- The zeros correspond. -/
  zeroK : R d₁.zeroK d₂.zeroK
  /-- The ones correspond. -/
  oneK : R d₁.oneK d₂.oneK
  /-- Addition of coefficients preserves the relation. -/
  addK : ∀ x y, R x y → ∀ z w, R z w → R (d₁.addK x z) (d₂.addK y w)
  /-- Multiplication of coefficients preserves the relation. -/
  mulK : ∀ x y, R x y → ∀ z w, R z w → R (d₁.mulK x z) (d₂.mulK y w)
  /-- Negation of coefficients preserves the relation. -/
  negK : ∀ x y, R x y → R (d₁.negK x) (d₂.negK y)
  /-- The zeros of the algebra correspond. -/
  zeroA : T d₁.zeroA d₂.zeroA
  /-- The ones of the algebra correspond. -/
  oneA : T d₁.oneA d₂.oneA
  /-- Addition in the algebra preserves the relation. -/
  addA : ∀ x y, T x y → ∀ z w, T z w → T (d₁.addA x z) (d₂.addA y w)
  /-- Multiplication in the algebra preserves the relation. -/
  mulA : ∀ x y, T x y → ∀ z w, T z w → T (d₁.mulA x z) (d₂.mulA y w)
  /-- Negation in the algebra preserves the relation. -/
  negA : ∀ x y, T x y → T (d₁.negA x) (d₂.negA y)
  /-- Related coefficients include to related constants. -/
  constA : ∀ x y, R x y → T (d₁.constA x) (d₂.constA y)
  /-- Related umbrae include to related elements. -/
  injA : ∀ u v, S u v → T (d₁.injA u) (d₂.injA v)
  /-- **Evaluation cannot separate related elements.** -/
  evalA : ∀ x y, T x y → R (d₁.evalA x) (d₂.evalA y)

/-! ## The free theorem of the signature, written out

`pabstraction_closed` delivers `PTyRel (quantTy body) …`, which is a
*recursion on the type*. `free_quantTy` is what that recursion computes, spelled
out, and its proof is the abstraction theorem applied to nothing but the term.
The two are therefore definitionally equal, so what follows is the free theorem
of the signature rather than a consequence of it.

This matters because `den_rel` below, like every theorem proved by
`exact ha x y h`, *applies* the relation to arguments, and an application can
lose strength. Reading the two side by side shows exactly what `den_rel` drops:
it instantiates the three type variables at base sorts, the same on both sides,
where the relation allows any `Ty Slot` and a different one on each side.
Heterogeneity survives anyway, because `Ib` and `Jb` differ. -/

/--
**The free theorem at `quantTy body`, written out.** Three instantiated type
variables, three arbitrary relations between them, and thirteen related
dictionary members give results related at the body.
-/
theorem free_quantTy (Ib Jb : Slot → Type)
    (body : PTy 3) (t : PClosedTm (quantTy body)) :
    ∀ (aK bK : Ty Slot) (RK : Rel (TyD Ib aK) (TyD Jb bK))
      (aU bU : Ty Slot) (RU : Rel (TyD Ib aU) (TyD Jb bU))
      (aA bA : Ty Slot) (RA : Rel (TyD Ib aA) (TyD Jb bA)),
      ∀ (zK₁ : TyD Ib aK) (zK₂ : TyD Jb bK), RK zK₁ zK₂ →
      ∀ (oK₁ : TyD Ib aK) (oK₂ : TyD Jb bK), RK oK₁ oK₂ →
      ∀ (adK₁ : TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aK)
        (adK₂ : TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bK),
        (∀ x y, RK x y → ∀ z u, RK z u → RK (adK₁ x z) (adK₂ y u)) →
      ∀ (mlK₁ : TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aK)
        (mlK₂ : TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bK),
        (∀ x y, RK x y → ∀ z u, RK z u → RK (mlK₁ x z) (mlK₂ y u)) →
      ∀ (ngK₁ : TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aK) (ngK₂ : TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bK),
        (∀ x y, RK x y → RK (ngK₁ x) (ngK₂ y)) →
      ∀ (zA₁ : TyD Ib aA) (zA₂ : TyD Jb bA), RA zA₁ zA₂ →
      ∀ (oA₁ : TyD Ib aA) (oA₂ : TyD Jb bA), RA oA₁ oA₂ →
      ∀ (adA₁ : TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aA)
        (adA₂ : TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bA),
        (∀ x y, RA x y → ∀ z u, RA z u → RA (adA₁ x z) (adA₂ y u)) →
      ∀ (mlA₁ : TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aA)
        (mlA₂ : TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bA),
        (∀ x y, RA x y → ∀ z u, RA z u → RA (mlA₁ x z) (mlA₂ y u)) →
      ∀ (ngA₁ : TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aA) (ngA₂ : TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bA),
        (∀ x y, RA x y → RA (ngA₁ x) (ngA₂ y)) →
      ∀ (cs₁ : TyD Ib aK → TyD Ib aA) (cs₂ : TyD Jb bK → TyD Jb bA),
        (∀ x y, RK x y → RA (cs₁ x) (cs₂ y)) →
      ∀ (ij₁ : TyD Ib aU → TyD Ib aA) (ij₂ : TyD Jb bU → TyD Jb bA),
        (∀ x y, RU x y → RA (ij₁ x) (ij₂ y)) →
      ∀ (ev₁ : TyD Ib aA → TyD Ib aK) (ev₂ : TyD Jb bA → TyD Jb bK),
        (∀ x y, RA x y → RK (ev₁ x) (ev₂ y)) →
      PTyRel body
        ((((REnv.nil Ib Jb).cons aK bK RK).cons aU bU RU).cons aA bA RA)
        (pevalC Ib t aK aU aA zK₁ oK₁ adK₁ mlK₁ ngK₁
          zA₁ oA₁ adA₁ mlA₁ ngA₁ cs₁ ij₁ ev₁)
        (pevalC Jb t bK bU bA zK₂ oK₂ adK₂ mlK₂ ngK₂
          zA₂ oA₂ adA₂ mlA₂ ngA₂ cs₂ ij₂ ev₂) :=
  pabstraction_closed Ib Jb t

/-! ## The free theorem, in the form the file uses -/

/--
**The free theorem of `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → K`.** Every expression of
this type sends related umbrae to related coefficients, for every triple of
relations respecting the class.

Everything else in this file is an instance of this one statement, obtained by
choosing the three relations.
-/
theorem den_rel {K₁ U₁ A₁ K₂ U₂ A₂ : Type}
    (d₁ : Dict K₁ U₁ A₁) (d₂ : Dict K₂ U₂ A₂)
    (R : K₁ → K₂ → Prop) (S : U₁ → U₂ → Prop) (T : A₁ → A₂ → Prop)
    (h : Respects d₁ d₂ R S T) (t : PClosedTm oneUmbraTy)
    (u₁ : U₁) (u₂ : U₂) (hu : S u₁ u₂) :
    R (den K₁ U₁ A₁ d₁ t u₁) (den K₂ U₂ A₂ d₂ t u₂) :=
  free_quantTy (baseD K₁ U₁ A₁) (baseD K₂ U₂ A₂) (PTy.tvar 1 ⇛ PTy.tvar 2) t
    (.base .K) (.base .K) R (.base .U) (.base .U) S (.base .A) (.base .A) T
    d₁.zeroK d₂.zeroK h.zeroK
    d₁.oneK d₂.oneK h.oneK
    d₁.addK d₂.addK h.addK
    d₁.mulK d₂.mulK h.mulK
    d₁.negK d₂.negK h.negK
    d₁.zeroA d₂.zeroA h.zeroA
    d₁.oneA d₂.oneA h.oneA
    d₁.addA d₂.addA h.addA
    d₁.mulA d₂.mulA h.mulA
    d₁.negA d₂.negA h.negA
    d₁.constA d₂.constA h.constA
    d₁.injA d₂.injA h.injA
    d₁.evalA d₂.evalA h.evalA
    u₁ u₂ hu

/-! ## The moment model as an instance of the class -/

variable {k : Type} [CommRing k] {Name : Type}

/-- The moment model: coefficients `k`, umbrae the names, algebra
`k[\text{names}]`, evaluation the umbral evaluation at `ρ`. -/
noncomputable def momDict (ρ : Assign Name k) :
    Dict k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) where
  zeroK := 0
  oneK := 1
  addK := (· + ·)
  mulK := (· * ·)
  negK := (- ·)
  zeroA := 0
  oneA := 1
  addA := (· + ·)
  mulA := (· * ·)
  negA := (- ·)
  constA := MvPolynomial.C
  injA := MvPolynomial.X
  evalA := eval ρ

/--
**An injective moment-preserving renaming respects the class.**

Thirteen clauses, of which twelve are the statement that renaming variables is a
ring homomorphism fixing the constants. The thirteenth is `eval_rename`, and it
is the only one that uses injectivity.
-/
theorem respects_momentMap [DecidableEq Name] {ρ₁ ρ₂ : Assign Name k}
    (σ : MomentMap ρ₁ ρ₂) :
    Respects (momDict ρ₁) (momDict ρ₂) (fun x y => x = y)
      (fun u v => σ.toFun u = v)
      (fun p q => MvPolynomial.rename σ.toFun p = q) where
  zeroK := rfl
  oneK := rfl
  addK := by rintro x y rfl z w rfl; rfl
  mulK := by rintro x y rfl z w rfl; rfl
  negK := by rintro x y rfl; rfl
  zeroA := map_zero _
  oneA := map_one _
  addA := by rintro x y rfl z w rfl; exact map_add _ _ _
  mulA := by rintro x y rfl z w rfl; exact map_mul _ _ _
  negA := by rintro x y rfl; exact map_neg _ _
  constA := by rintro x y rfl; exact MvPolynomial.rename_C _ _
  injA := by rintro u v rfl; exact MvPolynomial.rename_X _ _
  evalA := by
    rintro p q rfl
    exact (eval_rename σ p).symm

/-! ## The substitution principle -/

/--
**Rota's substitution principle, as a free theorem.**

Two umbrae with the same moment sequence are interchangeable in every definable
scalar-valued umbral expression. The relation at the umbra variable is the
transposition exchanging them, which respects the class precisely because they
are similar.
-/
theorem similarity [DecidableEq Name] (ρ : Assign Name k) {a b : Name}
    (hab : Similar ρ ρ a b) (t : PClosedTm oneUmbraTy) :
    den k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t a
      = den k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t b := by
  have h := den_rel (momDict ρ) (momDict ρ) _ _ _
    (respects_momentMap (swapMap ρ hab)) t a b
    (by simp [swapMap, Equiv.swap_apply_left])
  exact h

/-! ## What uncorrelation costs

If injectivity were part of the definition of a respecting triple, it would be
an assumption about which relations count. Here its failure has a price: a
relation that respects the moment dictionaries, with equality on scalars, and
relates two distinct umbrae `a` and `c` to the same umbra `b`, forces `E[aⁱ] E[cʲ] = E[b^{i+j}]`. Uncorrelation is not
derived here. It is definitional: `eval` is a sum over monomials of a product
over *distinct* variables. What is derived is the restriction it places on the
relations that respect the dictionary. -/

/-- The `n`-th powers of related polynomials are related. -/
theorem Respects.pow {ρ₁ ρ₂ : Assign Name k} {R : k → k → Prop} {S : Name → Name → Prop}
    {T : MvPolynomial Name k → MvPolynomial Name k → Prop}
    (h : Respects (momDict ρ₁) (momDict ρ₂) R S T) {x y : MvPolynomial Name k} (hxy : T x y) :
    ∀ n : ℕ, T (x ^ n) (y ^ n)
  | 0 => by simpa [momDict] using h.oneA
  | n + 1 => by simpa [pow_succ, momDict] using h.mulA _ _ (h.pow hxy n) _ _ hxy

/--
**Two umbrae related to one force multiplicative moments.**

Let `R_U` respect the moment dictionaries of `ρ₁` and `ρ₂`, with equality on
scalars, and relate the distinct umbrae `a` and `c` to the same umbra `b`. Then
`E[aⁱ] E[cʲ] = E[b^{i+j}]` for all `i` and `j`: `aⁱ cʲ` is related to `b^{i+j}`,
evaluation cannot separate them, and uncorrelation splits the left side.
-/
theorem moments_of_common_target {ρ₁ ρ₂ : Assign Name k} {S : Name → Name → Prop}
    {T : MvPolynomial Name k → MvPolynomial Name k → Prop}
    (h : Respects (momDict ρ₁) (momDict ρ₂) (fun x y => x = y) S T)
    {a c b : Name} (hac : a ≠ c) (hab : S a b) (hcb : S c b) (i j : ℕ) :
    moments ρ₁ a i * moments ρ₁ c j = moments ρ₂ b (i + j) := by
  have hprod := h.mulA _ _ (h.pow (h.injA a b hab) i) _ _ (h.pow (h.injA c b hcb) j)
  have heval := h.evalA _ _ hprod
  simp only [momDict] at heval
  rw [eval_X_pow_mul_X_pow ρ₁ hac, ← pow_add] at heval
  exact heval

/--
**Uncorrelation constrains the respecting relations.** If `a`, `c` and `b`
have the same moments, under `ρ₁` and `ρ₂`, and `aₙ ≠ a₁ⁿ` for some `n`, that is,
they are not the moments of the constant `a₁`, then no triple
respecting the moment dictionaries, with equality on scalars, relates both of
the distinct umbrae `a` and `c` to `b`.
-/
theorem no_common_target {ρ₁ ρ₂ : Assign Name k} {S : Name → Name → Prop}
    {T : MvPolynomial Name k → MvPolynomial Name k → Prop}
    (h : Respects (momDict ρ₁) (momDict ρ₂) (fun x y => x = y) S T)
    {a c b : Name} (hac : a ≠ c) (hab : S a b) (hcb : S c b)
    (hca : moments ρ₁ c = moments ρ₁ a) (hba : moments ρ₂ b = moments ρ₁ a)
    (hn : ∃ n, moments ρ₁ a n ≠ moments ρ₁ a 1 ^ n) : False := by
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := hn
  have key : ∀ i j, moments ρ₁ a i * moments ρ₁ a j = moments ρ₁ a (i + j) := by
    intro i j
    have := moments_of_common_target h hac hab hcb i j
    rwa [hca, hba] at this
  have hpow : ∀ n, moments ρ₁ a n = moments ρ₁ a 1 ^ n := by
    intro n
    induction n with
    | zero => simp [moments]
    | succ n ih => rw [show n + 1 = 1 + n by omega, ← key 1 n, ih]; ring
  exact hn (hpow n)

/-- An assignment for examples: every name carries the moment sequence
`a₀ = 1`, `a₁ = 0`, `aₙ = 1` for `n ≥ 2`. All names are therefore similar, and
`a₂ ≠ a₁²`. -/
def flat2 : Assign ℕ ℤ := fun _ n => if n = 1 then 0 else 1

@[simp] theorem moments_flat2 (a : ℕ) (n : ℕ) :
    moments flat2 a n = if n = 1 then 0 else 1 := by
  rcases eq_or_ne n 0 with rfl | hn
  · simp [moments]
  · rw [moments_eq _ _ _ hn]; rfl

/-! ## The language is inhabited

`moment1` is the first moment, `α ↦ eval[α]`. The de Bruijn indices count back
through the thirteen dictionary arguments and the umbra: `evalA` is one in,
`injA` two in, and the umbra is innermost. -/

open PTm in
/-- `Λ K U A. λ …dict… . λ u. evalA (injA u)` -/
def moment1 : PClosedTm oneUmbraTy :=
  tlam (tlam (tlam
    (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam (lam
      (app (var (.succ .zero)) (app (var (.succ (.succ .zero))) (var .zero)))
    ))))))))))))))))

theorem den_moment1 (ρ : Assign Name k) (a : Name) :
    den k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) moment1 a = moments ρ a 1 := by
  show eval ρ (MvPolynomial.X a) = moments ρ a 1
  rw [eval_X, moments_eq _ _ _ one_ne_zero]

/-- …and `similarity` says something about it: at `flat2` every name has the
same first moment, which the free theorem delivers without computing. -/
example (a b : ℕ) :
    den ℤ ℕ (MvPolynomial ℕ ℤ) (momDict flat2) moment1 a
      = den ℤ ℕ (MvPolynomial ℕ ℤ) (momDict flat2) moment1 b :=
  similarity flat2 (fun n => by simp) moment1

/-! ## Expressions in two umbrae: similar umbrae are exchangeable

The same free theorem at the body `U → U → K`. Reading it at the transposition of
two similar umbrae, applied to both arguments, says that an expression in two
similar umbrae is symmetric in them: the umbral form of the exchangeability of
independent, identically distributed random variables. -/

open PTy in
/-- `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → U → K`: a scalar-valued expression in two
umbrae. -/
abbrev twoUmbraTy : PTy 0 := quantTy (tvar 1 ⇛ tvar 1 ⇛ tvar 2)

/-- Read a closed term of `twoUmbraTy` at a dictionary. -/
def den2 (K U A : Type) (d : Dict K U A) (t : PClosedTm twoUmbraTy) :
    U → U → K :=
  pevalC (baseD K U A) t (.base .K) (.base .U) (.base .A)
    d.zeroK d.oneK d.addK d.mulK d.negK
    d.zeroA d.oneA d.addA d.mulA d.negA
    d.constA d.injA d.evalA

/-- **The free theorem at two umbrae.** Same proof as `den_rel`; only the body of
the quantified type differs. -/
theorem den2_rel {K₁ U₁ A₁ K₂ U₂ A₂ : Type}
    (d₁ : Dict K₁ U₁ A₁) (d₂ : Dict K₂ U₂ A₂)
    (R : K₁ → K₂ → Prop) (S : U₁ → U₂ → Prop) (T : A₁ → A₂ → Prop)
    (h : Respects d₁ d₂ R S T) (t : PClosedTm twoUmbraTy)
    (u₁ : U₁) (u₂ : U₂) (hu : S u₁ u₂) (v₁ : U₁) (v₂ : U₂) (hv : S v₁ v₂) :
    R (den2 K₁ U₁ A₁ d₁ t u₁ v₁) (den2 K₂ U₂ A₂ d₂ t u₂ v₂) :=
  free_quantTy (baseD K₁ U₁ A₁) (baseD K₂ U₂ A₂) (PTy.tvar 1 ⇛ PTy.tvar 1 ⇛ PTy.tvar 2) t
    (.base .K) (.base .K) R (.base .U) (.base .U) S (.base .A) (.base .A) T
    d₁.zeroK d₂.zeroK h.zeroK d₁.oneK d₂.oneK h.oneK
    d₁.addK d₂.addK h.addK d₁.mulK d₂.mulK h.mulK d₁.negK d₂.negK h.negK
    d₁.zeroA d₂.zeroA h.zeroA d₁.oneA d₂.oneA h.oneA
    d₁.addA d₂.addA h.addA d₁.mulA d₂.mulA h.mulA d₁.negA d₂.negA h.negA
    d₁.constA d₂.constA h.constA d₁.injA d₂.injA h.injA
    d₁.evalA d₂.evalA h.evalA u₁ u₂ hu v₁ v₂ hv

/--
**Similar umbrae are exchangeable.** An expression in two similar umbrae takes
the same value when they are exchanged. The relation at the umbrae is the
transposition exchanging them, applied to both arguments.
-/
theorem exchangeable [DecidableEq Name] (ρ : Assign Name k) {a b : Name}
    (hab : Similar ρ ρ a b) (t : PClosedTm twoUmbraTy) :
    den2 k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t a b
      = den2 k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t b a :=
  den2_rel (momDict ρ) (momDict ρ) _ _ _ (respects_momentMap (swapMap ρ hab)) t a b
    (by simp [swapMap, Equiv.swap_apply_left]) b a
    (by simp [swapMap, Equiv.swap_apply_right])

/-! ## Expressions in any number of umbrae: the substitution principle in full

An expression in `m` umbrae has the body `U → ⋯ → U → K`, with `m` arguments.
Its free theorem relates the values at two tuples of umbrae related pointwise.
Read at the transposition of two similar umbrae `a` and `b`, it gives the
substitution principle as the write-up states it: `a` may be replaced by `b` in
an expression whose other umbrae are all different from `a` and `b`. The
hypothesis that `b` is none of the other umbrae is the classical assumption that
`b` does not already occur. -/

open PTy in
/-- The body `U → ⋯ → U → K` with `m` umbra arguments, under the three
quantifiers. -/
def umbraArrows : ℕ → PTy 3
  | 0 => tvar 2
  | m + 1 => tvar 1 ⇛ umbraArrows m

/-- `∀K U A. Umbral K U A ⇒ U → ⋯ → U → K`: a scalar-valued expression in `m`
umbrae. -/
abbrev manyUmbraTy (m : ℕ) : PTy 0 := quantTy (umbraArrows m)

/-- Apply a curried function of `m` umbrae to a tuple, first entry first. -/
def applyN {Ib : Slot → Type} (η : Fin 3 → Ty Slot) :
    (m : ℕ) → PTyD Ib (umbraArrows m) η → (Fin m → TyD Ib (η 1)) → TyD Ib (η 2)
  | 0, f, _ => f
  | m + 1, f, u => applyN η m (f (u 0)) (fun i => u i.succ)

/-- The logical relation at `umbraArrows m` relates the values at pointwise
related tuples. -/
theorem applyN_rel {Ib Jb : Slot → Type} (ρ : REnv Ib Jb 3) :
    (m : ℕ) → (f : PTyD Ib (umbraArrows m) ρ.l) → (g : PTyD Jb (umbraArrows m) ρ.r) →
      PTyRel (umbraArrows m) ρ f g →
      (u : Fin m → TyD Ib (ρ.l 1)) → (v : Fin m → TyD Jb (ρ.r 1)) →
      (∀ i, ρ.rel 1 (u i) (v i)) →
      ρ.rel 2 (applyN ρ.l m f u) (applyN ρ.r m g v)
  | 0, _, _, h, _, _, _ => h
  | m + 1, f, g, h, u, v, huv =>
      applyN_rel ρ m (f (u 0)) (g (v 0)) (h (u 0) (v 0) (huv 0))
        (fun i => u i.succ) (fun i => v i.succ) (fun i => huv i.succ)

/-- Read a closed term of `manyUmbraTy m` at a dictionary, as a function of an
`m`-tuple of umbrae. -/
def denN (m : ℕ) (K U A : Type) (d : Dict K U A)
    (t : PClosedTm (manyUmbraTy m)) (u : Fin m → U) : K :=
  applyN (Ib := baseD K U A)
    (scons (.base .A) (scons (.base .U) (scons (.base .K) fun i => i.elim0))) m
    (pevalC (baseD K U A) t (.base .K) (.base .U) (.base .A)
      d.zeroK d.oneK d.addK d.mulK d.negK
      d.zeroA d.oneA d.addA d.mulA d.negA
      d.constA d.injA d.evalA) u

/-- **The free theorem at `m` umbrae.** Pointwise related tuples of umbrae give
related values, for every triple of relations respecting the class. -/
theorem denN_rel {K₁ U₁ A₁ K₂ U₂ A₂ : Type}
    (d₁ : Dict K₁ U₁ A₁) (d₂ : Dict K₂ U₂ A₂)
    (R : K₁ → K₂ → Prop) (S : U₁ → U₂ → Prop) (T : A₁ → A₂ → Prop)
    (h : Respects d₁ d₂ R S T) (m : ℕ) (t : PClosedTm (manyUmbraTy m))
    (u₁ : Fin m → U₁) (u₂ : Fin m → U₂) (hu : ∀ i, S (u₁ i) (u₂ i)) :
    R (denN m K₁ U₁ A₁ d₁ t u₁) (denN m K₂ U₂ A₂ d₂ t u₂) :=
  applyN_rel
    ((((REnv.nil (baseD K₁ U₁ A₁) (baseD K₂ U₂ A₂)).cons (.base .K) (.base .K) R).cons
      (.base .U) (.base .U) S).cons (.base .A) (.base .A) T) m _ _
    (free_quantTy (baseD K₁ U₁ A₁) (baseD K₂ U₂ A₂) (umbraArrows m) t
      (.base .K) (.base .K) R (.base .U) (.base .U) S (.base .A) (.base .A) T
      d₁.zeroK d₂.zeroK h.zeroK d₁.oneK d₂.oneK h.oneK
      d₁.addK d₂.addK h.addK d₁.mulK d₂.mulK h.mulK d₁.negK d₂.negK h.negK
      d₁.zeroA d₂.zeroA h.zeroA d₁.oneA d₂.oneA h.oneA
      d₁.addA d₂.addA h.addA d₁.mulA d₂.mulA h.mulA d₁.negA d₂.negA h.negA
      d₁.constA d₂.constA h.constA d₁.injA d₂.injA h.injA
      d₁.evalA d₂.evalA h.evalA)
    u₁ u₂ hu

/--
**Rota's substitution principle, in full.**

Let `a` and `b` be similar umbrae, and let `c₁, …, cₘ` be umbrae different from
both. Then replacing `a` by `b` in the last argument of an expression in `m + 1`
umbrae does not change its value. The hypothesis `c i ≠ b` is the classical
assumption that `b` does not already occur; the hypothesis `c i ≠ a` loses nothing,
since the expression may use its last argument wherever `a` occurs. The relation
at the umbrae is the transposition of `a` and `b`, which fixes every `c i`.
-/
theorem substitution [DecidableEq Name] (ρ : Assign Name k) {a b : Name}
    (hab : Similar ρ ρ a b) (m : ℕ) (t : PClosedTm (manyUmbraTy (m + 1)))
    (c : Fin m → Name) (hca : ∀ i, c i ≠ a) (hcb : ∀ i, c i ≠ b) :
    denN (m + 1) k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t (Fin.snoc c a)
      = denN (m + 1) k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t (Fin.snoc c b) :=
  denN_rel (momDict ρ) (momDict ρ) _ _ _ (respects_momentMap (swapMap ρ hab)) (m + 1) t
    _ _ (fun i => by
      refine Fin.lastCases ?_ (fun j => ?_) i
      · simp [swapMap, Equiv.swap_apply_left]
      · simp [swapMap, Equiv.swap_apply_of_ne_of_ne (hca j) (hcb j)])

/-! ## Integrality: a free theorem at a relation that is not a graph

Take the relation at the scalars to be the diagonal of the subring generated by
the moments, and at the polynomials the diagonal of the polynomials whose
coefficients lie in it. Both are closed under the operations, and the evaluation
maps the second into the first. The free theorem then says that every
expression lands in the subring generated by the moments. -/

/-- The subring generated by all the moments of an assignment. -/
def momentSubring (ρ : Assign Name k) : Subring k :=
  Subring.closure (Set.range fun x : Name × ℕ => ρ x.1 x.2)

theorem moment_mem (ρ : Assign Name k) (a : Name) (i : ℕ) :
    ρ a i ∈ momentSubring ρ :=
  Subring.subset_closure ⟨(a, i), rfl⟩

/-- **The subring diagonals respect the class.** The clause with content is the
evaluation: `eval ρ p` is a sum of coefficients times products of moments. -/
theorem respects_subring (ρ : Assign Name k) :
    Respects (momDict ρ) (momDict ρ)
      (fun x y => x = y ∧ x ∈ momentSubring ρ) (fun u v => u = v)
      (fun p q => p = q ∧ ∀ d, p.coeff d ∈ momentSubring ρ) where
  zeroK := ⟨rfl, Subring.zero_mem _⟩
  oneK := ⟨rfl, Subring.one_mem _⟩
  addK := by
    rintro x _ ⟨rfl, hx⟩ z _ ⟨rfl, hz⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, Subring.add_mem _ hx hz⟩
  mulK := by
    rintro x _ ⟨rfl, hx⟩ z _ ⟨rfl, hz⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, Subring.mul_mem _ hx hz⟩
  negK := by
    rintro x _ ⟨rfl, hx⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, Subring.neg_mem _ hx⟩
  zeroA := ⟨rfl, fun d => by simp [momDict]⟩
  oneA := ⟨rfl, fun d => by
    classical
    simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.coeff_one]
    split_ifs
    · exact Subring.one_mem _
    · exact Subring.zero_mem _⟩
  addA := by
    rintro p _ ⟨rfl, hp⟩ q _ ⟨rfl, hq⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, fun d => by
      simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.coeff_add]
      exact Subring.add_mem _ (hp d) (hq d)⟩
  mulA := by
    rintro p _ ⟨rfl, hp⟩ q _ ⟨rfl, hq⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, fun d => by
      classical
      simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.coeff_mul]
      exact Subring.sum_mem _ fun x _ => Subring.mul_mem _ (hp x.1) (hq x.2)⟩
  negA := by
    rintro p _ ⟨rfl, hp⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, fun d => by
      simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.coeff_neg]
      exact Subring.neg_mem _ (hp d)⟩
  constA := by
    rintro x _ ⟨rfl, hx⟩
    exact ⟨rfl, fun d => by
      classical
      simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.coeff_C]
      split_ifs
      · exact hx
      · exact Subring.zero_mem _⟩
  injA := by
    rintro u _ rfl
    exact ⟨rfl, fun d => by
      classical
      simp only [momDict, MvPolynomial.X, MvPolynomial.coeff_monomial]
      split_ifs
      · exact Subring.one_mem _
      · exact Subring.zero_mem _⟩
  evalA := by
    rintro p _ ⟨rfl, hp⟩
    refine ⟨rfl, ?_⟩
    simp only [momDict, eval]
    refine Subring.sum_mem _ fun d _ => Subring.mul_mem _ (hp d) ?_
    exact Subring.prod_mem _ fun a _ => moment_mem ρ a (d a)

/-- **Integrality.** The value of every expression lies in the subring generated
by the moments: it is an integer polynomial in them. -/
theorem integral (ρ : Assign Name k) (t : PClosedTm oneUmbraTy) (a : Name) :
    den k Name (MvPolynomial Name k) (momDict ρ) t a ∈ momentSubring ρ :=
  (den_rel (momDict ρ) (momDict ρ) _ _ _ (respects_subring ρ) t a a rfl).2

end FreeThms.Umbral.Restricted
